WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.720
"He could have added fortune and fame, but
caring for neither, he found happiness and

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.420
honor in being helpful to the world."

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:12.339
Those words are found on the gravestone of
George Washington Carver, an inspirational

00:00:12.339 --> 00:00:17.070
scientist who was once dubbed by TIME Magazine
as the “Black Leonardo.”

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:22.380
This was apropos as Carver regarded himself
more of an artist who used natural things

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.519
rather than a true scientist.

00:00:24.519 --> 00:00:29.140
“Cook-stove chemists” was how he jokingly
referred to himself and his research team.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:33.380
At a time of high racial tensions, when he
was part of a community that was still heavily

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:38.980
marginalized and discriminated against, the
talent and achievements of George Carver transcended

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.030
all color barriers and were appreciated by
the entire nation.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:46.660
And, despite all this, most simply remember
him as the “Peanut Man.”

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:51.610
His work with peanuts brought him the most
success, although even by Carver’s own admission,

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:52.910
it was not his greatest work.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:57.390
But everything he did, he did with a purpose
- to help “the furthest man down.”

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:02.980
Today, we take a look at the full extent of
the career and legacy of George Washington

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:05.360
Carver.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:12.490
Early Years &amp; Education

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:19.120
George Washington Carver was born in a small
town called Diamond Grove in Newton County,

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.570
Missouri, today known simply as Diamond.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:23.560
His birth date is a mystery.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.810
Because he was born into slavery, the exact
date was never recorded.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:32.440
It was sometime during the mid 1860s, but
before slavery was abolished in January 1865.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:37.790
Later, Carver said that it happened “near
the end of the war” so 1864 is generally

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:40.000
given as his year of birth.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.250
Right from the very beginning, Carver experienced
great tragedy and we’re talking about more

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.790
than the obvious misery of being born a slave.

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:50.780
His parents were Mary and Giles and they were
owned by a German-American immigrant named

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:52.120
Moses Carver.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:56.940
His father died before he was born and when
George was just an infant, he, his mother,

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:02.090
and older sister were kidnapped by bushwackers
and taken to be sold in Arkansas.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:06.729
Moses Carver hired a man to get them back,
but he was only able to track down and recover

00:02:06.729 --> 00:02:08.750
George in exchange for a horse.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.890
Afterwards, slavery may have been abolished,
but the boy was still an orphan.

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:16.860
Fortunately for him, Moses Carver and his
wife Susan took in George and his older brother

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:19.420
James and raised them as their own.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.350
George was a frail child and was often sick
during his childhood.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:26.600
Therefore, he was too weak to work on the
farm with Moses and James so, instead, he

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:30.980
helped Susan Carver around the home by doing
chores, cooking, and tending the garden.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:33.590
During their free time, Susan taught him to
read and write.

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:37.730
He showed an intellectual aptitude from a
young age and his adoptive guardians encouraged

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.670
him to pursue an education.

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:41.940
Around the age of 12, George wanted to enroll
in school.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:45.910
However, he couldn’t do that in Diamond
Grove because it only had one public school

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:47.430
and it did not accept black pupils.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:51.209
Instead, he traveled to the county seat of
Neosho about ten miles away.

00:02:51.209 --> 00:02:54.069
From this point on, he left the Carver farm,
more or less.

00:02:54.069 --> 00:02:58.430
He would sometimes return and stay for a weekend
but, other than that, he kept moving from

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:00.470
one place to another to further his education.

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:05.220
It was also in Neosho that he first started
going by “George Carver” as, up until

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.420
that point, he kept referring to himself as
“Carver’s George.”

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:12.580
The “Washington” he added later in honor
of a man who played an important role in his

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:13.580
life.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:17.080
Due to discrimination, Carver had to jump
from one school to the next before finding

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:18.080
acceptance.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:22.020
He eventually managed to obtain a high school
diploma after graduating from Minneapolis

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.709
High School in Minneapolis, Kansas.

00:03:24.709 --> 00:03:26.610
From there, George wanted to move on to college.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:31.400
After being rejected by most of the institutions
he applied for, Carver was finally accepted

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:36.310
sight unseen by Highland College in Kansas,
but they, too, later rejected him once they

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:37.700
discovered that he was black.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:42.489
Feeling a bit discouraged, George gave up
his dream of going to college for a while

00:03:42.489 --> 00:03:47.349
and, instead, claimed a homestead which he
farmed alone, while simultaneously doing odd

00:03:47.349 --> 00:03:50.110
jobs and working as a ranch hand to support
himself.

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:55.060
He did this for a few years but, by the late
1880s, Carver was ready to move on to something

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:56.060
else.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:00.269
He managed to secure a loan from a bank and
he left Kansas, making his way to Iowa.

00:04:00.269 --> 00:04:04.530
In the city of Winterset, he met and befriended
a white couple called the Milhollands who

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:06.990
urged him to continue his higher education.

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:11.370
Although reluctant at first, Carver eventually
relented and enrolled in a little Methodist

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:14.989
school in Indianola, Iowa, called Simpson
College.

00:04:14.989 --> 00:04:19.759
The time he spent there had a deep effect
on George Carver, not just from a career perspective,

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:22.079
but also from a spiritual one.

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:26.589
It greatly strengthened his Christian faith
as he finally found a place which accepted

00:04:26.589 --> 00:04:32.400
everyone regardless of race and made him “believe
[he] was a real human being.”

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:37.029
Carver studied a multitude of subjects at
Simpson College but, somewhat unexpectedly,

00:04:37.029 --> 00:04:39.370
focused mainly on art, particularly painting.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:43.349
This had been a passion of his ever since
he was a young man, but his teacher, Etta

00:04:43.349 --> 00:04:48.240
Budd , did not think that a black man in that
day and age had a chance of making a decent

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:49.389
living as a painter.

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:53.279
When she found out that Carver was also interested
in plants, she encouraged him to switch to

00:04:53.279 --> 00:04:58.969
botany and enroll at the Iowa State Agricultural
College in Ames where her father taught horticulture.

00:04:58.969 --> 00:05:05.719
Going to Tuskegee

00:05:05.719 --> 00:05:10.580
In 1891, George Carver became the first black
man accepted at Iowa State University.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:14.659
Now fully living his dream, he embraced all
facets of campus life.

00:05:14.659 --> 00:05:18.650
Carver was on the debate club, the athletics
team, he was captain of the campus military

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:22.650
regiment, he wrote poetry published in the
student newspaper and two of his paintings

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:26.229
were even exhibited at the Chicago World’s
Fair in 1893.

00:05:26.229 --> 00:05:30.819
But, of course, it was his agricultural work
that earned him the most praise.

00:05:30.819 --> 00:05:36.119
After presenting his thesis in 1894 titled
“Plants as Modified by Man,” Carver graduated

00:05:36.119 --> 00:05:38.490
and continued to study for his master’s
degree.

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:42.430
His professors were so impressed with his
work that they asked him to begin teaching,

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:43.430
as well.

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:47.949
His master’s program saw Carver work as
assistant botanist for the College Experiment

00:05:47.949 --> 00:05:53.569
Station, focusing on plant pathology (which
is the study of disease in plants) and mycology

00:05:53.569 --> 00:05:55.460
(which is the study of fungi).

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:59.569
During this time, he wrote several papers
on his work that were published and started

00:05:59.569 --> 00:06:03.779
garnering him a reputation as one of the country’s
leading young scientists.

00:06:03.779 --> 00:06:06.710
He completed his master’s degree in 1896.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:10.789
Now that his education was complete, Carver
was ready to embark on a permanent career

00:06:10.789 --> 00:06:12.770
as teacher and researcher.

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:18.930
He wasn’t hurting for offers but, in the
end, he chose a young institution in Tuskegee,

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:21.120
Alabama, that had only been established 15
years prior.

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:26.339
It was the Tuskegee Institute, known today
as Tuskegee University, the passion project

00:06:26.339 --> 00:06:31.440
of prominent civil rights activist Booker
T. Washington . Widely influential and respected

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:36.259
among the elite circles of American society,
Washington wanted to set up a university that

00:06:36.259 --> 00:06:41.000
could provide a higher education for black
people without fear of discrimnation.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:45.580
He founded the Tuskegee Institute in 1881
with the help of donations from industrialists

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:49.110
such as Andrew Carnegie and John D. Rockefeller.

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:54.050
Around the mid 1890s, Washington had entered
the second phase of his vision for his university

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:59.129
which was to recruit the most talented black
teachers and scientists in the country.

00:06:59.129 --> 00:07:02.979
Obviously, Carver was on his list and he clearly
shared Washington’s vision.

00:07:02.979 --> 00:07:08.259
In 1896, Carver moved to Alabama where he
accepted a position with the Agriculture Department

00:07:08.259 --> 00:07:12.319
at the Tuskegee Institute, one he would keep
for the rest of his life.

00:07:12.319 --> 00:07:17.169
You might expect that the pairing of Carver
and Washington to be a match made in heaven,

00:07:17.169 --> 00:07:19.749
but the two actually butted heads quite often.

00:07:19.749 --> 00:07:22.770
Those who knew them believed they had opposing
personalities.

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:27.400
Washington was a pragmatist who set a goal
and tried to accomplish it as fast as possible.

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:32.199
Carver, on the other hand, was more of a dreamer
who liked to fritter the time away in his

00:07:32.199 --> 00:07:34.949
lab, either painting or looking after his
plants.

00:07:34.949 --> 00:07:40.069
As the director of the newly created Agricultural
Experiment Station, George Carver expected

00:07:40.069 --> 00:07:43.470
his job to mostly involve research which was
what he was interested in.

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:49.229
However, Washington also wanted him to manage
the farms, teach classes, and serve on several

00:07:49.229 --> 00:07:50.580
committees and councils.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:55.050
It was the bureaucracy and all the paperwork
that Carver loathed most of all.

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:58.819
He threatened to quit several times during
Washington’s tenure as President of the

00:07:58.819 --> 00:07:59.979
Tuskegee Institute.

00:07:59.979 --> 00:08:04.969
The latter always managed to smooth things
over, but he also flat-out refused to give

00:08:04.969 --> 00:08:09.149
into some of Carver’s requests such as giving
up his teaching duties.

00:08:09.149 --> 00:08:13.899
Despite their adversarial professional relationship,
the two had a healthy respect for each other

00:08:13.899 --> 00:08:16.179
and always spoke highly of one another.

00:08:16.179 --> 00:08:21.069
Carver would later even add “Washington”
to his name in honor of the civil rights leader.

00:08:21.069 --> 00:08:25.639
Besides his spats with the university leadership,
Carver wasn’t exactly popular with the other

00:08:25.639 --> 00:08:28.689
faculty members when he first came in, either.

00:08:28.689 --> 00:08:32.070
Many of them resented the privileges that
Washington offered him in order to get him

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:33.510
to come to Tuskegee.

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:38.599
This was still in the infancy stages of the
university which did not have a lot of space

00:08:38.599 --> 00:08:40.030
or money to go around.

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:44.720
Other faculty members had to live two in one
dormitory room if they were unmarried and

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:47.020
they made, on average, $400 a year.

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:52.410
Carver, on the other hand, had an annual salary
of $1,000 plus expenses and got two dormitory

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:55.610
rooms all to himself.

00:08:55.610 --> 00:09:00.370
How to Improve Your Crops

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:04.520
Despite the various administrative obstacles
that Carver encountered during his time at

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:08.770
Tuskegee, they did not hamper his effectiveness
as a scientist or researcher.

00:09:08.770 --> 00:09:13.020
He decided that his efforts would best be
spent helping the farmers of America, particularly

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:18.110
in the southern states because Carver believed
that they would suffer in the years and decades

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:20.780
that followed due to their over-reliance on
cotton.

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:24.750
The practice of growing the same crop in the
same place year after year after year is called

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:25.750
monocropping.

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:29.630
It is a problem because it depletes the soil
of the same nutrients over and over again

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:34.080
and leads to soil erosion which, in turn,
will result in much poorer crop yields in

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:35.080
the future.

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:39.250
Cotton, for example, is quite a demanding
crop that uses up a lot of nitrogen in order

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:40.250
to grow.

00:09:40.250 --> 00:09:44.720
To alleviate the issue of monocropping, farmers
sometimes practiced crop rotation which simply

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:48.960
involved alternating between different crops
for subsequent growing seasons so that the

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:51.640
soil had time to build up its nutrients again.

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:55.540
George Carver was a big proponent of crop
rotation, but he did not invent it, just to

00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:56.540
be clear.

00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:59.910
It had been practiced since ancient times,
but farmers from the American South had no

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:04.370
interest in it because they regarded cotton
as the only cash crop around.

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:06.680
Anything else would not have been anywhere
near as useful and, therefore, would not have

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:07.810
made as much money.

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:12.030
At first, Carver presented other ideas, such
as suggesting that farmers use commercial

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:17.620
fertilizer with a high nitrogen content or
that they buy a second horse and use a two-horse

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:19.870
plow which would till the soil deeper.

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:24.060
These suggestions were seen as a bit tone-deaf,
coming from someone who didn’t truly grasp

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:26.411
the situation in the South.

00:10:26.411 --> 00:10:29.900
It wasn’t that farmers were unaware of these
solutions, or that they were ignorant to the

00:10:29.900 --> 00:10:34.820
problems caused by monocropping, but that
they did not have any money to do anything

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:35.820
about it.

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:39.750
The farmers that Carver aimed to help were
all poor, and many of them were black.

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:43.700
Following the abolition of slavery, wealthy
landowners gave them parcels of land which

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:47.820
they could work in exchange for a set fee
or a percentage of the crop.

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:52.510
Their profit margins were razor thin and even
just one bad growing season could cause them

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:55.390
to lose everything and plunge them into crippling
debt.

00:10:55.390 --> 00:10:59.470
They had very few land rights and were up
against a system which was completely stacked

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:00.470
against them.

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:04.410
It was a “system of near slavery,” as
one historian described it.

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:08.450
Therefore, since the land was never truly
theirs, anyway, the farmers had almost no

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:12.120
incentive to care about the health of the
soil, let alone spend whatever money they

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:14.430
saved up in order to improve it.

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:19.130
Carver soon realized the extent of the issues
that plagued the poor southern farmers so

00:11:19.130 --> 00:11:24.440
his research focused on other ways to improve
their work that did not necessarily require

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:25.980
any serious investments.

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:30.790
In this regard, almost all of the ideas he
had while working at Tuskegee were published

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:32.500
in the form of bulletins.

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:36.570
He issued 44 of these bulletins between 1898
and 1943.

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:40.950
The first one, for example, was titled “Feeding
Acorns” and it simply encouraged farmers

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:46.170
to use acorns to fatten up their livestock
as a cheaper alternative to corn that, otherwise,

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:47.330
typically went to waste.

00:11:47.330 --> 00:11:53.120
Carver’s most popular bulletin was No. 31
from 1916 which saw multiple reprintings.

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:57.320
Titled “How to Grow the Peanut &amp; 105 Ways
of Preparing It for Human Consumption,”

00:11:57.320 --> 00:12:02.320
it was a list of recipes collected from other
cookbooks, magazines, newspapers, and agricultural

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:03.320
bulletins.

00:12:03.320 --> 00:12:07.310
Carver’s educational efforts did not consist
solely of these bulletins.

00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:11.920
He still felt that his information was not
reaching all of its intended audience, mainly

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:16.030
because a lot of the farmers were illiterate
or simply because they were not expecting

00:12:16.030 --> 00:12:19.100
any kind of actual help from an official institution.

00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:23.800
Carver decided that he needed a more direct
way to bring education to these people and,

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:27.130
thus, the Jesup Agricultural Wagon was born.

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:32.060
Founded in 1906 by George Carver, this was
the first step in the Movable School outreach

00:12:32.060 --> 00:12:36.670
program of the Tuskegee Institute and was
named after New York banker Morris Ketchum

00:12:36.670 --> 00:12:38.920
Jesup who funded the program.

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:43.410
These wagons would travel from farm to farm
with equipment and instructional charts and

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:47.810
give lectures to farmers on how to improve
the quality of their soil and alternate cotton

00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:48.940
with other crops.

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:52.650
It proved successful enough that the United
States Department of Agriculture eventually

00:12:52.650 --> 00:12:55.620
adopted it for their own outreach program.

00:12:55.620 --> 00:13:01.320
The Peanut Man

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:05.280
In order to convince farmers to willingly
switch out their cotton with other crops,

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:09.870
Carver needed to show them that it was possible
for them to make a living by growing other

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:10.870
things.

00:13:10.870 --> 00:13:14.610
Thus he embarked on a new mission which was
to research and develop new applications for

00:13:14.610 --> 00:13:16.830
crops that he felt had been overlooked.

00:13:16.830 --> 00:13:20.610
Of course, we all know about the most famous
one of all - the peanut.

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:25.010
In the minds of many, finding 300 uses for
the peanut is still Carver’s main claim

00:13:25.010 --> 00:13:26.010
to fame.

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:30.290
One of the main reasons why the agricultural
scientist focused on this legume was because

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:34.760
the peanut was quite adept at repairing the
damage done to the soil by cotton.

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:39.320
Not only did it have meager nitrogen needs,
but it could also grow with relatively little

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:40.480
water consumption.

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:45.920
As we already mentioned, 105 of those 300
uses were food recipes which he presented

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:47.620
in his most popular bulletin.

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:52.290
They included: flour, chili sauce, dry coffee,
instant coffee, chop suey sauce, mayonnaise,

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:57.480
meat loaf, salad oil, buttermilk, mock chicken,
mock duck, mock goose, vinegar, caramel, cheese

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:00.920
pimento, peanut wafers, and many, many more.

00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:04.930
Of course, that was just the tip of the peanutty
iceberg.

00:14:04.930 --> 00:14:10.190
Carver also showed that the legume could be
used to make various types of feed for livestock,

00:14:10.190 --> 00:14:12.430
plus a dozen different types of beverages.

00:14:12.430 --> 00:14:16.800
He used it for medicines such as rubbing oils,
tonics, and laxatives; and numerous cosmetics

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:22.160
such as shampoo, hand &amp; face lotions, shaving
cream, soap, pomade, and dandruff cures.

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:24.810
The peanut was useful around the house, as
well.

00:14:24.810 --> 00:14:28.930
Carver turned it into laundry soap, leather
dyes, paints, wood stains, insecticide, various

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:32.160
types of paper, metal polish, diesel fuel
and gasoline, rubber, ink, linoleum, axle

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:33.670
grease, lubricating oil, and insulating board.

00:14:33.670 --> 00:14:39.100
As you can see, the peanut was an all-purpose,
practical crop, but almost none of these ideas

00:14:39.100 --> 00:14:40.100
actually took off.

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:41.100
Some of them had already been around for a
while.

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:44.980
After all, Carver made no pretense to have
come up with all of them, nor did he argue

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:47.630
that they were superior to their alternatives.

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:48.630
That was not the point.

00:14:48.630 --> 00:14:53.310
Carver’s goal was to show southern farmers,
and the country as a whole, that it was possible

00:14:53.310 --> 00:14:56.540
to develop other cash crops apart from cotton.

00:14:56.540 --> 00:15:00.490
Given that Carver’s hundreds of uses for
peanuts never became very popular, it is a

00:15:00.490 --> 00:15:04.360
little strange that the two are so inexorably
linked.

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:08.290
Historians point the finger at an obscure
event from Carver’s life when he gave an

00:15:08.290 --> 00:15:10.740
impassioned testimony before Congress.

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:15.160
It was the moment that permanently connected
the scientist to the legume in the public

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:20.460
mind, but then the event itself was forgotten
so, nowadays, people remember the connection,

00:15:20.460 --> 00:15:21.960
but not its origins.

00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:23.230
Back in 1921, the U.S.

00:15:23.230 --> 00:15:26.140
Government wanted to place a tariff on imported
peanuts.

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:30.580
Carver testified before the U.S. House Ways
and Means Committee and gave a dazzling speech

00:15:30.580 --> 00:15:34.730
to support the tariff in order to bolster
the nation’s own growth of peanuts.

00:15:34.730 --> 00:15:39.130
The tariff was passed and, afterwards, Carver
became somewhat of a media sensation.

00:15:39.130 --> 00:15:43.540
One more thing that should be specified about
George Washington Carver’s nutty career

00:15:43.540 --> 00:15:45.730
is that he did not invent peanut butter.

00:15:45.730 --> 00:15:50.070
This might be a common belief, but it is incorrect
as peanut butter already existed by the time

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:51.980
he started publishing his bulletins.

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:56.880
In fact, the oldest references to the salty
snack go all the way back to the Aztecs and

00:15:56.880 --> 00:16:00.720
the Incas who made a paste out of ground up
roasted peanuts.

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:04.900
The first to make modern peanut butter was
Canadian chemist Marcellus Gilmore Edson who

00:16:04.900 --> 00:16:08.470
patented peanut-candy, as he called it, in
1884.

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:13.450
The process was later refined by John Harvey
Kellogg of Kellogg’s cereal fame and, in

00:16:13.450 --> 00:16:19.190
1903, Dr. Ambrose Straub invented a machine
especially designed to make peanut butter.

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:24.330
Legacy &amp; Last Years

00:16:24.330 --> 00:16:28.560
According to Carver himself, his research
into peanuts was not the greatest work of

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:29.560
his career.

00:16:29.560 --> 00:16:34.110
Even so, he never actively discouraged anyone
from making the association between the two

00:16:34.110 --> 00:16:38.130
which is why he is still so closely associated
with the legume even today.

00:16:38.130 --> 00:16:41.420
He didn’t mind the fame that he enjoyed
in the last decades of his life after his

00:16:41.420 --> 00:16:43.110
testimony before Congress.

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:47.770
He believed that he could use it to advance
his causes, to promote the Tuskegee Institute

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:49.930
and to encourage more racial integration.

00:16:49.930 --> 00:16:54.540
Carver’s research into sustainable farming
ended up saving countless farmers from ruin

00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:59.210
when the boll weevil ravaged the southern
cotton crops at the start of the 20th century.

00:16:59.210 --> 00:17:03.740
This ravenous pest made its way from Mexico
around 1892 and, in just a couple of decades,

00:17:03.740 --> 00:17:08.149
turned into a major threat not only to all
the cotton crops in the country, but the economy

00:17:08.149 --> 00:17:09.919
which was completely reliant on them.

00:17:09.919 --> 00:17:14.730
This “wave of evil,” as it was dubbed
by the USDA, did not succeed in destroying

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:20.350
the financial state of the South thanks to
George Carver and others like him who advocated

00:17:20.350 --> 00:17:22.220
for crop diversification.

00:17:22.220 --> 00:17:28.600
Peanut plantation expanded from 500,000 acres
in 1916 to over four million acres in 1918

00:17:28.600 --> 00:17:30.129
and continued growing from there.

00:17:30.129 --> 00:17:34.470
The city of Enterprise, Alabama, even has
a monument dedicated to the boll weevil, the

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:38.919
first such structure in the world made to
honor an agricultural pest.

00:17:38.919 --> 00:17:43.629
They dubbed the insect a “herald of prosperity”
and were grateful that it forced the locals

00:17:43.629 --> 00:17:48.010
to switch to growing peanuts instead of cotton
which triggered a period of affluence for

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:49.010
the city.

00:17:49.010 --> 00:17:50.010
And it wasn’t just peanuts.

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:54.779
Although the legume was Carver’s bread and
(peanut) butter, if you will, he saw the problem

00:17:54.779 --> 00:17:58.980
in trying to prevent monocropping by simply
switching one crop with another crop.

00:17:58.980 --> 00:18:03.620
Therefore, he also advocated for the cultivation
of other plants such as soybeans and pecans.

00:18:03.620 --> 00:18:07.009
He was a big supporter of the sweet potato,
almost as big as the peanut.

00:18:07.009 --> 00:18:11.250
He saw many similarities between them in terms
of the benefits they would provide to cotton

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:13.480
farmers and how easy they were to grow.

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:17.230
While Carver wasn’t as imaginative with
the sweet potato, he still found over a hundred

00:18:17.230 --> 00:18:18.230
uses for the root vegetable.

00:18:18.230 --> 00:18:19.230
They included flour, sugar, starch, vinegar,
over a dozen varieties of candy, yeast, dry

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:20.230
&amp; instant coffee, sauce, hog feed, alcohol,
ink, paper, dyes, paints, and synthetic silk.

00:18:20.230 --> 00:18:23.260
Some argue that George Carver’s true legacy
was that of a pioneering environmentalist.

00:18:23.260 --> 00:18:28.610
There is no doubting that the ideas he promoted
about sustainability and self-sufficiency

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:33.590
had an impact on the world around him, perhaps
more significant, if not more well-known,

00:18:33.590 --> 00:18:35.039
than his work with peanuts.

00:18:35.039 --> 00:18:39.590
In his later years, Carver served as agricultural
advisor to three U.S. Presidents.

00:18:39.590 --> 00:18:42.919
He counselled Gandhi on matters of nutrition
and agriculture.

00:18:42.919 --> 00:18:46.169
He taught Henry Ford how to make peanut rubber
during World War II.

00:18:46.169 --> 00:18:50.840
It might be a little hard to pinpoint exactly
what George Washington Carver should be remembered

00:18:50.840 --> 00:18:54.559
for, but when he talked, people listened.

00:18:54.559 --> 00:18:59.070
He died on January 5, 1943, approximately
79 years old.

00:18:59.070 --> 00:19:02.701
Afterwards, there was talk of establishing
a national monument in his honor but, due

00:19:02.701 --> 00:19:07.750
to World War II, all such non-essential expenditures
were banned by presidential order.

00:19:07.750 --> 00:19:12.249
However, a senator from his home state of
Missouri sponsored a bill to make an exception

00:19:12.249 --> 00:19:15.720
in order “to do honor to one of the truly
great Americans.”

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:20.679
That senator was future President Harry S.
Truman and his bill was passed in both houses

00:19:20.679 --> 00:19:35.419
unanimously.

